EXPERIMENT 6
Configure a Site-to-Site IPsec VPN in Cisco Packet Tracer
Step-by-step practical chapter | Source: PDF pages 25–39
1. Aim
Configure IPsec on two routers to protect traffic between two LANs.
2. Tools Required
Cisco Packet Tracer; two Cisco 2911 routers; two PCs; copper straight-through and crossover/automatic connections.
3. Before You Begin
Follow the steps in order. Keep a record of commands, settings, output, and screenshots required by your instructor. Use only systems and networks you own or have permission to test.
4. Procedure
Step 1: Build the topology
1.	Open Cisco Packet Tracer and place two 2911 routers (Router0 and Router1) and two PCs (PC0 and PC1).
2.	Connect PC0 FastEthernet0 to Router0 GigabitEthernet0/0 using a straight-through cable.
3.	Connect Router0 GigabitEthernet0/2 to Router1 GigabitEthernet0/2 using crossover or Automatically Choose Connection Type.
4.	Connect Router1 GigabitEthernet0/0 to PC1 FastEthernet0 using a straight-through cable.
Step 2: Configure PC addressing
5.	PC0 → Desktop → IP Configuration: IP `192.168.1.10`, subnet mask `255.255.255.0`, default gateway `192.168.1.1`.
6.	PC1 → Desktop → IP Configuration: IP `192.168.2.10`, subnet mask `255.255.255.0`, default gateway `192.168.2.1`.
Step 3: Configure Router0 interfaces and route
7.	Click Router0 → CLI. Enter the commands below. If prompted for the initial configuration dialog, answer `no` and press Enter.
! Router0
enable
configure terminal
interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
 ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit
interface gigabitEthernet 0/2
 ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252
 no shutdown
exit
ip route 192.168.2.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.2
access-list 110 permit ip 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255
crypto isakmp policy 10
 encryption aes
 hash sha
 authentication pre-share
 group 5
exit
crypto isakmp key CIS123 address 10.0.0.2
crypto ipsec transform-set VPN-SET esp-aes esp-sha-hmac
crypto map VPN-MAP 10 ipsec-isakmp
 set peer 10.0.0.2
 set transform-set VPN-SET
 match address 110
exit
interface gigabitEthernet 0/2
 crypto map VPN-MAP
end
write memory

! Router1 (enter separately on Router1)
enable
configure terminal
interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
 ip address 192.168.2.1 255.255.255.0
 no shutdown
exit
interface gigabitEthernet 0/2
 ip address 10.0.0.2 255.255.255.252
 no shutdown
exit
ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.1
access-list 110 permit ip 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255
crypto isakmp policy 10
 encryption aes
 hash sha
 authentication pre-share
 group 5
exit
crypto isakmp key CIS123 address 10.0.0.1
crypto ipsec transform-set VPN-SET esp-aes esp-sha-hmac
crypto map VPN-MAP 10 ipsec-isakmp
 set peer 10.0.0.1
 set transform-set VPN-SET
 match address 110
exit
interface gigabitEthernet 0/2
 crypto map VPN-MAP
end
write memory

Step 4: Configure Router1 interfaces and route
8.	Click Router1 → CLI and enter the Router1 commands below.
Step 5: Configure the IPsec policy on both routers
9.	On Router0, configure the ACL, ISAKMP policy, pre-shared key, transform set and crypto map using the Router0 block below.
10.	On Router1, configure the mirrored ACL and the same policy/transform set, but set the peer to Router0's transit IP `10.0.0.1`.
11.	Apply the crypto map to each router's transit interface GigabitEthernet0/2. The pre-shared key and policy settings must match at both ends.
Step 6: Verify connectivity and tunnel
12.	First check interface status with `show ip interface brief` on each router.
13.	From PC0 Command Prompt, run `ping 192.168.2.10`; from PC1, run `ping 192.168.1.10`. The first ping may trigger tunnel negotiation; retry if needed.
14.	On the routers, run `show crypto isakmp sa` and `show crypto ipsec sa`. Look for an established IKE security association and increasing encapsulation/decapsulation counters.
Step 7: Save the configuration
15.	On each router, run `write memory` or `copy running-config startup-config`.
